A||N||24||Viper||Wondering if I should buy?????????????||||4||
Z||000000||Viper||01-03-2000||02:21 AM||||Hi, I'm a new member, Viper.....from the good 'ol west US.<P>I have a few questions for all you Fiero experts out there........maybe someone can help me out.<P>I'm about to get my first car, and it might as well be a Fiero....my brother thinks the Insurance rates will be too high, my mom thinks it's old, and some others think it might be unreliable, but GOD!!, I just love the car, and I want one..<P>I've read alot of messages already posted, and it sounds like the Fiero is a great car to have, and it makes me all more excited.  I've heard the handling and turning is not soo good, maybe someone can enlighten me.<P>I'm looking into a 1988 Base Coupe (4-banger)<BR>w/ 90,500 miles, Auto Tranny.....they say it runs well and is in good condition, it is white with a Spoiler and comes with a CD player.  I've emailed asking more questions about it...<P>It is selling for $ 1,800 (obo)<P>I'm wondering is this a good price?<P>Should I look for a 6-cyl. model? (remember this is my first car)<P>I'm 16 and am very anxious at working on the car myself, will that be entirely possible, intelligence and everything shouldn't be a problem, but is it a fairly easy car to work on?<P>I have other questions but it's late at night, I hope you Fiero lovers can answer me<P>P.S. (what kind of things should I look for and what kind of questions should I ask about the car??)<P><BR>-Thanks a bunch everyone!!!<P>-Viper (Derek)||||reg||4
Z||000001||Smokey||01-03-2000||03:12 AM||||lets see if i can help ya out here. as far as insurance goes...I only pay $60 dollars a month for my fiero, so i put back 15 bucks a week so i have it when i need it. As far as it being unreliable, you have to remember..these cars are at a minimum of like 12 years old, so you probably will encounter some problems somewhere along the line. The only thing I've ever had to put into mine was general maintainence like plugs, wires, cap, rotor, oil changes...stuff like that and an altenator. Are they easy to work on? it is quite a tight space to work in. when i bought my first fiero i thought they would be a pain to work on, but after actually doing it I found it was easier than it looked. Handiling not good? you might as well smack the person who told you that! to me 1800 dollars seems a little steep to me, but i've never seen the car, so i cant tell you that. What i can tell you is that i see Fiero GT's selling around here for 1700 in good condition, so i guess thats why 1800 seems steep to me for a 4 banger. 90,000 miles doesnt seem that bad to me for the cars age, but the question is just what kind of miles are they? are they highway miles or are they some teenage kids hotrod miles...you have to find out these things for yourself, but i hope i helped in some small way. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Shiner||01-03-2000||05:43 AM||||Whoever told you that fieros don't handle well is an idiot, and has obviously never driven one.  I have never driven a car that handles as good as my '87 GT.  My father has also driven many, many sports cars of the fiero's era, and he said it was one of the best handling cars he had ever driven.  <P> The '88 fieros are technicaly better cars.  The suspension was upgraded, and many small things were fixed.  The problem is that parts for the '88 cars are harded to find, and are more expensive when you do find them.  <P> No offense to you 4cyl/auto fiero owners out there (Hey, I have one!), but this trans/engine combo is not, ahem, extremely fast.  Okay, it's a pig (flame suit on).  You will probably want a manual trans later, they are ten times more fun, at least.  <P> I hope you find the perfect fiero for yourself!  And just be warned: you can't have just one  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000003||ChadMan||01-03-2000||10:34 AM||||I'd buy it, Viper.  $1800 isn't unreasonable if the car is in excellent condition...if it isn't, haggle them down.<P>I bought an 88 Coupe in EC (68K miles) for $2500 in 1996.  I put a clutch, rear springs, shocks and struts in it when I bought it.  It now has 106K miles on it and has NEVER broken down or failed to start(knock on wood).  It has been the most reliable car I've ever owned.  Hopefully you will have the same luck.<P>A few things to look for...<P>1)  Does the "Service Engine Soon" instrument-panel light work?  Unscrupulous sellers sometimes remove the bulb to mask problems with emissions/electrical systems.<BR>2)  How's driveability?  Does it idle roughly, hesitiate on acceleration, etc.?  These problems can usually be fixed fairly cheaply, but you shouldn't pay top $$ for a car that doesn't run perfectly.<BR>3)  88 Coupes have weak rear springs (I replaced mine with Formula springs).  Stand behind the car and look at the rear wheels.  If they appear to lean in, or the inner edges of the rear tires are worn, haggle the seller down and plan on installing springs.<BR>4)  Does the auto trans' lockup torque converter work?  That's the only common trouble spot I know of with the THM-125 transmission.<P>To be sure, the 4-cyl auto isn't fast, but you won't have to worry about your clutch going bad (which all Fiero clutches seem to).  Let us know if you buy it!||||reg||1
Z||000004||turbo86se||01-03-2000||11:09 AM||||Viper, sounds like a good deal!  Get it! You'll love the 88 suspension. I have an 88 coupe, one with rare T-Tops, with 115k miles. The shocks and struts have never been changed. If you push down on the car, it bounces up and down like an old Buick!! LOL  When driving the car, man it handles like it's on rails!! I've got a low-mileage Formula V6 sitting in my garage just begging to be installed!!  I can't wait to see how this car performs with new balljoints, shocks and struts, and that V6! <BR>Chadman, that spring change is a good idea. I know the front springs are different from 84-87 Fieros on 88's, but are the rear springs different?  I've got a 85SE V6 parts car. The rear springs on that car should be stiffer than what I've got on there now.  Would this combination work?  Any suggestions are welcome! ||||reg||1
Z||000005||fieroshop||01-03-2000||01:05 PM||||Viper, if you need help I can check out the Fiero for you. I see that you live close by. I am in Hayward.||||reg||6
Z||000006||ka4nkf||01-03-2000||01:15 PM||||I have Had both the four cyl and the six cyl. I would try to find the six cyl if i were you. There is no comparsing between the four and the six. Yhe fiero is a great car it just needs TLC.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tom88gt||01-03-2000||02:49 PM||||Hold on there.  $1800 for a 4 cyl is way too much, unless it's showroom perfect or has t-tops.  <P>4 cyl usually didn't get the proper care (if the first owners were too cheap for a V-6 or did'nt care about performance, then they probably didn't care too much about maintenance).  On the flip side, V-6s were generally abused by teenagers.<P>If you want a sports car you will probably prefer a stick over an auto (here, personnal preference is key).<P>Don't even touch it if it doesn't have AC.  Not even worth installing, even if the car is free (unless it's a t-top car).<P>All 88s have the new suspension, and brakes.  The 4 cyl is missing gertrag 5 speed plus the rear sway bar and heavier rear springs (both with can just be bolted on.  But a modified and maintained 84-87 can probably beat an old 88 suspension (only a side beside test drive by yourself will tell you for sure).  Yes, the 88s are a few more $ to repair but not enough to really matter.<P>Look for the "fierotrend internet magazine" on the web.  Get info on inspecting any used fiero.<P>If the car checks out, offer $500 and keep looking while the seller is thinking about your offer.  I guarrantee if you buy it for $1,800 you will see one next week for $500 or so.  There were just too many built, too much bad press, and too poor resale as a result.<P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000008||ChadMan||01-03-2000||03:41 PM||||Aw, Tom, don't say such nasty things about 4-bangers where my 88 Coupe can hear you - you'll hurt its feelings.  If I had passed up my Coupe, I would have missed out on 4 years of cheap transportation and 3 FOCOA trophies (granted, the competition isn't nearly as tough as the GT or Formula classes).<P>You're right about the V6 cars being a lot more fun (I sure miss mine).  But the 4-bangers make great daily drivers, they get good mileage, and they're easier to work on.  And what else would a 16-year-old find in this price range?  A rusty Honda?  Some old lady's Cutlass Ciera?  A (gulp) K-car?||||reg||9
Z||000009||DRH||01-03-2000||05:18 PM||||Viper,<P>"Unreliable" is usually caused more by poor maintenance than years. If it has been reasonably well maintained and you are willing to give it the TLC a 12+ year old car needs... it should as reliable as any other car you could get for that kind of money.<P>Insurance should not be a major factor. Most companies don't consider it a sports car. The GT may run a little more at your age. Call your parent's company and check.<P>A V6 5spd is more fun to drive. A 4 Auto is probably the best choice for a first car...  cheaper to buy, insurance might be a little better, you can tear up the clutch on someone else's car learning a stick, AND you are a lot less likely to get in trouble (tickets) with it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>As far as price... they seem to vary a lot by condition and location. $1800 is probably not out of line in Silicon Valley if it is in good shape. As with ANY used car, offer less!!! most sellers build in negotiation room. If you can work out the logistics I think having Fieroshop check it out is a great idea.<P>Despite what you will hear from some shops... the Fiero is NOT any harder to work on than most other cars from the 80's.<P>Good Luck, David.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||87GT||01-03-2000||08:28 PM||||Hey Viper,  I noticed that you said you were going to be doing most of the repair work on your fiero.  I have had all my maintenance done by my cousin who is a mechanic.  He services Ferraris, Porsches, Saabs, you name it.  The last time I went over there to pick up my car he said that of all the cars he has ever had to work on, the Pontiac Fiero is the hardest to work on.  So, good luck if you do most of the work yourself.  BTW, $1800 doesnt sound that bad if it is in decent shape.  c-ya and enjoy||||reg||1
Z||000011||mrfiero||01-04-2000||04:12 AM||||Turbo86SE,<P>All 4 springs on the '88 Fiero are different from those on '84-'87 Fieros, so the rear springs from your '85 SE will not work on an '88.<P>The earlier years rear springs are wider (for lack of a better description) than the '88s.||||reg||1
Z||000012||ChadMan||01-04-2000||10:15 AM||||Yeah, turbo, mrfiero's right.  I think all the 88 springs are firmer than the 84-87 because the suspension arms are longer.  (I know what I mean, I just don't know how to say it...)||||reg||1
Z||000013||DJRice||01-04-2000||10:28 AM||||I still maintain that a Fiero is not any harder to work on then your average FWD vehicle from the 80s...  It does help to have small arms, but all in all, the 4 cyl should be easy to work on.<P>Good luck and enjoy.||||reg||1
Z||000014||hercules||01-04-2000||01:24 PM||||ALERT---We have a crazy Viper, I repeat...ALERT!!!!!!!  <BR>Viper, dont do it! $1,800 is way to much for a four banger.  I have 2 fieros.  The first is an '86 GT Fastback with an auto tranny and every option except t-tops, and I paid $900 for it.  It only needed regular work like brakes exhaust etc.  The second one I have is an '86 notchback 4 cyl. 5 spd. with no options except tilt, and that one is in excelent condition. It only needed brakes and a good tune-up, it had been sitting a while.  I paid $200 for it.  If $1800 is what you have to spend, look for an '88 Fomula with the lotus suspension, getrag 5 spd and better brakes for around $1200 and keep the additional money aside for any repairs you might need.||||reg||13
Z||000015||HF6582||01-04-2000||01:39 PM||||lotus suspension?  I guess my 86 has lab diablo suspension!!  Lotus had nothing to do with the suspension.<P>Scott||||reg||8
Z||000016||hercules||01-04-2000||01:46 PM||||oops, sorry!  I still wouldnt buy it for $1800!||||reg||1
Z||000017||ChadMan||01-04-2000||02:01 PM||||Where do you guys keep finding these phenomenal deals on Fieros?  In the paper?  On the Net?  Because all I seem to find are three types of Fieros:<P>1)  Really nice ones that the owners want an arm and a leg for<BR>2)  Beat-up or broken ones that the owners will sell cheap<BR>3)  Beat-up or broken ones that the dumba$$ owner still wants top dollar for||||reg||5
Z||000018||Tom88gt||01-04-2000||02:02 PM||||Sorry Chad,  but I didn't say 4s were bad.  Just less likely to have gotten the proper care by their previous owners.  <P>Viper,<BR>I hear a lot of contrasting opinions on price.  As with every car, the car itself is the determining factor.  It's condition.  <P>But as a general rule, start with Kelly's Blue Book value (don't use the NADA consumer edition - a total farce to help car dealers rip off the consumer).  The only valid price it the trade in/loan value.  If you ever had to sell the car or it was wrecked/stolen, you will not get more than this value.  Unless you had certified appraisals, maybe.<P>I think a universal standard is that any car which runs good, everything works and is in good shape is worth a least $1000.  Reguardless of make/model.  I've heard that said by countless people.<P>Due to the unique "reputation" of the fiero you will find good cars for sale below trade-in value and below the $1000 mark.  I'm sorry guys, but our beloved fieros must all fight this uphill battle, which will probably never end.  <P>It is worth noting that the cars owned by members of this forum and other fiero organizations obviously take pride in their fieros.  Therefore, the care and maintenance these cars need is all but guarranteed to have been provided.  Thus "forum" fieros are worth more than an "unkown".  <P>In other words, don't just consider the car, consider the seller.||||reg||12
Z||000019||PAUL88GT||01-04-2000||02:22 PM||||I just sold an 86 2M4 (125kmiles on body, less on engine) for $1350. Body was OK except for a few sun-faded areas.||||reg||1
Z||000020||william_thorniii||01-04-2000||02:56 PM||||Chadman,<P>It seems that everyone that thinks that $1800 is much too high is from CA (or similar).  In the rest of the country, $1800 is not a terrible price for that car.  I bought a 1987 SC L-4 with 48,000 last september for $1900.  I have only had to replace the alt. and belt in the 25,000 that I have put on it. <P><BR>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000021||Raydar||01-04-2000||03:19 PM||||Okay... I paid $2000 dollars for an 88 4cyl/automatic coupe, two years ago. Did I pay too much? Yeah, probably. But it *was* what I wanted (an 88). And it was in good condition. You may not be satisfied with the 4 cyl, but it's a good place to start. (BTW... in GA, $1200-$2000 is normal "ask" price for an 88 coupe. Formulas go for $2000+. Even the ratty ones. GTs are rare and getting expensive.)||||reg||1
Z||000022||hercules||01-04-2000||04:22 PM||||As far as where to buy them, I live just outside Boston, and the most reliable and constant place I use is the WANT AD.  Im not sure who gets it or what areas it is available, but prices range from $200-$4500 that i've seen.  I've also had great luck with just finding them in backyards and sitting in driveways.  I know of 2 that are for sale in my town and 1 in a town 5 min. from me.  I also know of a guy in Braintree, MA that has about 6 and they are all for sale well below $1800.  They range from 4cyl, 6cyl, 1 fastback, 2 formulas etc.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Formula||01-05-2000||06:24 PM||||There is still a near mint looking 86 GT for sale for around $2000 in western PA.  Its a beauty!||||reg||1
Z||000024||GT Bastard||01-05-2000||06:44 PM||||I agree with William.  Why is everyone pi$$ing and moaning about the money?  I hear everyone saying they found a 4-banger fiero for .02 cents and there are 10 more down the street for a dollar.  I know that I've seen a lot of fieros for sale and i have yet to see one in any condition under 700 bucks(and it was ratty as hell.)  Viper, if the car is in your budget and you like it, buy it!  I would suggest checking around for other prices in the area too, but 1800 bucks isn't bad.  I'm sure you could get it for 1500 pretty easily.  Money isn't everything.||||reg||1
